## Is it safe to leave the car at Afonso Pena Airport? The short answer

In almost every case, yes. Afonso Pena International Airport, IATA code CWB, sits in São José dos Pinhais, in the Curitiba metropolitan area. The lots around it operate with a controlled gate, a camera circuit and staff on site. Several have accumulated hundreds or thousands of public reviews on Google.

But secure parking at Curitiba Airport is not proved with a badge, it is proved with something verifiable. The four questions that matter most: who controls entry, who watches the cameras, what the actual staffing hours are and what goes in writing when the car arrives.

In short: it is safe to leave the car at Afonso Pena Airport at a lot that controls access, records the entry with plate and time, and is staffed at the hour of your flight. If a lot does not answer those four questions, its problem is not the price.

## Why does this question about Curitiba end up on Reddit?

Anyone typing whether it is safe to leave the car at Afonso Pena Airport has almost always read three commercial pages first. All of them said the same thing: a secure environment, 24-hour monitoring, a trained team. None said how many cameras, who watches, or what happens if a mirror comes back broken.

So the person goes looking for a first-hand account on a forum, in a travel group and in Google's review tab. They do not want an opinion, they want to know what happened to someone who went through that gate.

This text assumes that from the start. Nothing here is praise for a lot: it is what you can check yourself, in a few minutes, plus what the law guarantees afterwards.

## What does the law say if the car is damaged in Curitiba?

The legal answer is the part a sales page does not carry, and it is the one that most reassures anyone leaving the car in Curitiba.

### What does the STJ's Precedent 130 say?

The Superior Court of Justice established in 1995 that a company answers to its customer for damage to or theft of a vehicle occurring in its car park. That is Precedent 130. It is the basis of almost every claim over a car damaged or taken from a paid lot.

The detail that matters: the liability is strict. You do not have to prove the car park was careless. It is enough to prove you used the service and that the damage happened while the car was in its care. That is why the entry receipt is worth so much: it proves safekeeping existed.

### What limit did the STJ itself place on it?

The precedent is not a blank cheque. In 2019, the STJ ruled on a robbery in an open, free, freely accessible car park. There was no duty to compensate there, because there was neither safekeeping nor a charge, only a convenience ([decision published by the STJ itself](https://www.stj.jus.br/sites/portalp/Paginas/Comunicacao/Noticias/2019/Roubo-em-estacionamento-aberto-e-de-livre-acesso-nao-gera-responsabilidade-para-o-comerciante.aspx)).

The practical reading for a traveller: a paid lot, with a gate and a record, is safekeeping. A street space and an uncontrolled plot are not. Leaving the car on a nearby street to save the daily rate takes you out of the protection entirely.

### What does the Consumer Protection Code add?

Two articles complete the picture. Under article 14, the supplier answers for a defect in the provision of a service, and vehicle safekeeping is a service. Under article 30, an offer binds: what was advertised becomes part of the contract ([full text on the Planalto portal](https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/leis/l8078.htm)).

Translating article 30: if the lot's page advertises cameras and a 24-hour gate, that stops being advertising and becomes an obligation. Save a screenshot before travelling. It costs ten seconds and it is evidence.

## What should you verify before leaving the car at Afonso Pena Airport?

Five checks on car park security in Curitiba, all quick, all done before leaving home.

### Monitoring: is anyone watching the cameras live?

The phrase "24-hour monitoring" fits on any leaflet, and it does not answer whether it is safe to leave the car at Afonso Pena Airport at that lot. The question that changes the result is another: is someone watching live, or does the system only record for later consultation?

Both serve, for different purposes. A recording resolves things after the fact, because it proves what happened and when. Live monitoring tries to prevent the fact. Ask which of the two the lot operates on and how many days the recording is kept. Thirty days is a common and comfortable figure for anyone travelling two weeks.

### Access control: is there a gate and a receipt?

With no control at the entrance, any car comes and goes. With a gate and a record, every entry and every exit is marked with a time.

It is that record that settles the argument on your return. It says what time the car came in, what time it left and under which booking. A lot that controls access answers that in a minute. With no control, it becomes your word against theirs.

### Hours: is the lot staffed when your flight lands?

Afonso Pena concentrates domestic flights and has traffic early in the morning and late at night, which is when this detail shows. A lot with 24-hour staffing takes you in at 5am without drama. A lot that closes the office at night may still release your exit, but has nobody to sign an incident report.

Check two separate things: whether the lot operates 24 hours and whether the transfer runs 24 hours too. They do not always go together.

### Cover: what does it protect against?

A covered space protects against sun, rain and hail. It does not protect against theft, and it is not what answers whether it is safe to leave the car at Afonso Pena Airport.

In Curitiba it weighs more than the national average, because the city has frequent rain and the winter punishes rubber and paintwork. On the 27 October 2026 table, the difference between the uncovered and the covered space at the same lot is R$ 49.00 over seven days at Abbapark, from R$ 153.30 to R$ 202.30. Beyond that, cover is preservation, not protection of property.

### Entry receipt: what does it need to carry?

A printed receipt, a record in the app or an email with the time. Any of them serves, as long as it exists and carries the plate, the date and the time. It is the document that underpins car park security in Curitiba when something goes wrong.

It is that document that proves safekeeping, and it is what Precedent 130 asks for behind the scenes. With no receipt, the discussion becomes an account. With a receipt, it becomes a record.

